Ajoute la spécification normative **Symbolic Intelligence Configuration (SIC)** pour l’architecture V8 de Logos Engine.

La version 0.3 fixe notamment :
- les cinq perspectives Perceptions, Informations, Stratégies, Objectifs et Plans d’actions ;
- les besoins typés comme couture transversale ;
- la mission comme contrat de finalité, distinct des objectifs actifs ;
- **Assimilation, Exploration et Solving** comme stratégies racines issues de la V5 ;
- exactement **un token de concentration**, avec les attentes stockées en graphe et présentées sous forme d’arbre ;
- un profil épistémique sans score global de confiance opaque ;
- une configuration déclarative bornée par défaut et des extensions procédurales Turing-complètes sous contrats explicites ;
- un **cadre de situation** révisable qui qualifie morphologie, topologie, contrôle, dynamique, contraintes et phase ;
- un **graphe contextuel de résolution par type de besoin**, afin qu’un même besoin puisse être traité différemment selon la situation ;
- une applicabilité ternaire : `applicable`, `not_applicable` ou `unknown` ;
- des enveloppes d’applicabilité avec préconditions, contre-indications, preuves minimales et portée ;
- des relations explicites de spécialisation, composition, séquence et fallback entre stratégies ;
- la détection à la compilation des chevauchements non ordonnés et des trous de couverture ;
- une matrice contextuelle de non-régression pour éviter qu’une stratégie ajoutée pour un jeu ne contamine un autre jeu portant le même besoin ;
- six niveaux de vérification, de la syntaxe à la preuve assistée ;
- des responsabilités humaines cumulables pour créer, vérifier, promouvoir, exploiter et auditer ;
- la création et la promotion des contrats comme processus de développement gouverné ;
- les frontières, versions, permissions et dépendances entre Intelligence Packs ;
- une migration progressive V8-0 à V8-12, d’abord en shadow puis sous feature flag.

Le changement reste exclusivement documentaire et ne modifie aucune décision du runtime courant.

## Objetivo
Aplicar transversalmente o conceito já aprovado na seção **Produtos**: cantos discretamente arredondados (6–12 px), linhas finas, superfícies neutras, laranja de destaque, controles proporcionais e hierarquia sem poluição visual.

## Mapeamento pelo Graphify (ArlesHub)
Workspace `saas` consultado; componentes-chave: `AdminApp`, `CatalogManager`, `ShopvaxDesignSystem`, `PublicStoreV2`, `SocialFeed`, `SocialDiscover`, `SocialShopping`, `SocialStoreProfile`, `ShopvaxDashboard` e `ShopvaxOrders`.

## Escopo da mudança
- Tokens visuais transversais de raio, borda, superfície e foco;
- Painel lojista (navegação, produtos, categorias, seleção, dashboards, relatórios e configurações);
- Catálogos (cartões, diálogos e botões);
- Vitrine e catálogo público (cards, filtros, busca e carrinho);
- Feed da loja e feed social (controles discretos sem recortar mídia/avatares);
- Descoberta, salvos, carrinhos, perfil de loja, modal de compra e conta do comprador;
- Login, planos, landing page, plataforma/admin e parceiros;
- Responsividade, foco por teclado e movimento reduzido.

## Restrições preservadas
Sem alteração de TypeScript/rotas/regras comerciais/API/DB. Não sobrescreve a cor configurada pelo lojista, não altera mídia original e não muda fluxo de WhatsApp/checkout. A nova folha é importada no final de `src/main.tsx` para consolidar a cascata.

## Critérios de validação antes do merge
- [ ] `npm run build` e CI no PR;
- [ ] Capturas reais desktop e 320 / 390 / 430 px do feed, perfil, descoberta, vitrine, catálogos, painel e login;
- [ ] Conferir overlays de compra, popovers, filtros, seleção e rolagem;
- [ ] Validar contraste, tabulação, foco visível, cor de loja e overflow;
- [ ] Aprovação visual e gate de publicação; sem alegar publicação em produção.

**Status:** proposta em branch de revisão, sem deploy.

## What
Slice 5a4 `cube-style`, an art experiment in rounds with user gates (spec `specs/cube-style.md`). The units stay cubes, as an art choice. This PR is the spec and **round 1, the stage**:

- **Style registry** in `presentation/demo/style/` (outside the ruleset roots, so tuning never changes the ruleset version):
  - a look per unit (exact, tag rule, default; rim, rim tint, roughness, colour override);
  - a `StageMood` per chapter (Environment with AgX tonemap, glow, SSAO and fog, key, fill and rim lights, floor and wall materials);
  - exploration and battle camera framings (today's camera is kept; Lyra-style camera modes are a later slice);
  - variants over a base for each round's comparisons.
- **System code** (`game/view/`): `StyleRegistry`, `UnitLook`, `UnitLookRule`, `StageMood`, `MoodLight`, `CameraFraming`, `StageRig`, `UnitStyle` (loads nothing headless); `UnitAnimator.body_material()` / `body_color()` / `restyle()`.
- **Room view and unit view** (small edits): the chapter's mood and the look's rim light. With no registry they look exactly like today.
- **Dev tools**:
  - console `style list | show | mood | registry | camera | reload | check`;
  - `shot <name> [seconds]`;
  - `StyleValidator` (`style_*` codes) in `validate_content.tscn`;
  - round variants left out of the player build.
- **Design** (approved at the round 1 gate): the default mood and 8 chapter moods with white key lights.

## Why
Cubes on a flat grey stage looked like a placeholder. Round 1 makes them read as a deliberate style through lighting, a mood per chapter and a rim light, at zero art cost per unit. Round 2 (attachments built from primitives: hats, crowns, horns, wings) comes next.

## Tests
- Godot suite: 798 passed, 0 failed, incl. the new `test_cube_style`:
  - resolution order and base merging;
  - the camera tween;
  - the stage rig;
  - the stencil stamp kept on styled bodies;
  - nothing headless, nothing in `combat/`, nothing under `presentation/` in the ruleset;
  - validator codes;
  - the console command;
  - the demo registry and variants validate.
- `validate_content.tscn`: 0 errors, 0 warnings.
- Review: 3 rounds of `code-review high`. Findings fixed; one design question is left open (should a variant's default look override a per-unit look in its base?).
- No .NET changes.

